  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 125 PM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nRidging aloft migrates into the Northern Rockies and brings \ncontinued clearing skies tonight, along with some patchy AM fog \nover the wind protected valleys of southwest and north-central MT.\nH500 heights peak above 580 dm and warms high temperatures well \ninto the 60s and 70s Friday and Saturday. The combination of \nincreased westerly flow aloft and deep layer mixing will also \nbring gusty to locally strong winds along the Rocky Mountain Front\nand the plains during this timeframe. The strongest winds will \noccur Saturday afternoon when H700 flow increases to the 50 to 70 \nkt range in advance of a fast approaching trough traveling \neastward along the Canadian border. Forecast afternoon RH's were \nlowered to the upper teens to middle 20s for most lower elevation \nlocations.\n\nA more relaxed zonal flow sets up on Sunday for slightly cooler \ntemperatures and lighter winds. A closed mid-level low then looks \nto move onto the Pacific NW coast on Monday before heading \nsoutheastward into California and towards the Great Basin area by \nmi- week. This will bring a southerly flow aloft and a few passing\nshowers over the Northern Rockies while temperatures remain \nroughly near average. \n\nAt least half of the ensemble suites highlight another trough \napproaching the Northern Rockies next Friday/Saturday. Similar to \nthe aforementioned closed low, this new trough will be undergoing \nshearing and splitting, which decreases forecast position and \ntiming confidence. Overall, there's a general expectation for \ncooling temperatures and increased instances of rain and mostly \nmountain snow heading into next weekend. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nGusty to strong winds along the Rocky Mountain Front and the \nplains Friday and Saturday...\n\nBreezy to windy conditions develop along the Rocky Mountain Front\nand the plains Friday afternoon, with localized gusts over 50 mph\nalong the immediate eastern slopes of the front. The windiest \nperiod will be Saturday afternoon when westerly flow aloft \nincreases and diurnal mixing transfers some stronger gusts to the \nsurface. Areas along the Rocky Mountain Front and plains west of \nI15 look to see routine gusts over 50 mph and have a 50 to 80% \nchance (highest for the eastern slopes of the front) for \ninstantaneous gusts over 70 mph. Plains locations to the east of \nI15 will generally see winds gusting in the 30 to 50 mph range, \nmost frequently Saturday afternoon. \n\nImpacts will be greatest for the aforementioned areas west of I15\nand will include difficult conditions for outdoor recreation over\nportions of Glacier National Park and neighboring areas of higher\nterrain. There will also be difficult travel for those operating \nhigh profile vehicles or pulling light weight trailers, especially\nalong highway 89 from Chouteau to the Canadian border. The \ninherited high wind watch remains valid and was left as is for \nthis forecast package. - RCG",
